\C{kitty-config} KiTTY's own configuration options

This chapter documents the settings KiTTY adds to PuTTY's configuration
box. Everything upstream PuTTY offers is described in
\k{config}; what follows is the fork's own, panel by panel, in the
order the panels appear.

Settings that belong to a \e{saved session} are stored with that session
and travel with it through \q{Export all} / \q{Import all}. Settings
that belong to the \e{installation} are stored once for this copy of
KiTTY, in \c{kitty.ini} or in the registry, and apply to every session;
those live on the \q{Application} tab (\k{kitty-cfg-application}) and
each one says so.

\H{kitty-cfg-session} The Session panel

\S{kitty-cfg-folders} \ii{Session folders}

A saved session can be filed in a \e{folder}, so a list of two hundred
machines becomes a handful of groups. The folder is a property of the
session: set it when you save, and the session appears under that name
in the list.

The list shows folders as a drop-down by default, and can show them as
rows instead - see \k{kitty-cfg-sessionlist}. \q{Default} is the folder
a session gets when you do not choose one; it is not a special folder
beyond that.

Deleting a folder does not delete the sessions in it. They return to
\q{Default}, because a session that belongs to nothing cannot be
listed.

\S{kitty-cfg-proxy-override} \q{Proxy override options}

Picks one of the named proxy definitions (\k{kitty-cfg-named-proxies})
for this session, without copying its settings into the session. The
session records \e{which} definition it uses, so changing the
definition changes every session that names it - a jump host that moves
to a new address is edited once.

\q{None} uses the session's own \q{Connection > Proxy} settings as
usual. The droplist appears only when at least one definition exists,
and can be hidden entirely (\k{kitty-cfg-sessionlist}).

\S{kitty-cfg-old-sessions} \q{Old sessions}

Shown only when this machine has sessions in an older KiTTY's registry
hive (\c{9bis.com}) or in PuTTY's, and this KiTTY had none of its own
when it first ran. Those sessions are listed alongside your own and can
be opened, edited and deleted; the button opens
\k{kitty-cfg-migration}, where they can be imported properly.

The behaviour is controlled by \c{showforeignsessions} in
\c{kitty.ini}: \c{auto} (the default) shows them only while this KiTTY
has no sessions of its own, so they retire themselves once you have
your own; \c{yes} and \c{no} settle it either way.

\S{kitty-cfg-hide-launcher} \q{Hide this session from the launcher}

Keeps this session out of the KiTTY Launcher's tray menu
(\k{kitty-cfg-launcher-hotkey}). The session is otherwise unchanged: it
still appears in the configuration box and still opens from the command
line. Use it for the sessions that exist as building blocks - a jump
host, a template - rather than as somewhere you connect.

\H{kitty-cfg-logging} The Logging panel

PuTTY's own logging options are described in \k{config-logging}. KiTTY
adds a timestamp for each logged line, and rotation.

\S{kitty-cfg-log-timestamp} \q{Timestamp (strftime format)}

Written at the start of every logged line. The value is a
\i{strftime} pattern, so the layout is yours: \c{%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S }
gives \c{2026-08-12 15:31:55 }, and \c{%d.%m.%Y %H:%M:%S: } gives
\c{12.08.2026 15:31:55: }. In addition to the standard strftime codes,
\c{%f} inserts \i{milliseconds}.

Leave the field empty and no timestamps are written, so logs look
exactly as they did before.

Timestamps apply to the session logs - \q{Printable output} and \q{All
session output}. The SSH packet and raw-data logs are deliberately left
alone: they already timestamp every record in their own format, which
other tools parse.

The button under the field fills in a working pattern when the field is
empty and clears it when it is not, so the feature can be tried and
undone without knowing strftime. Its label says which it will do.

\S{kitty-cfg-log-rotation} \q{Automatic logrotation}

Starts a new log file every N seconds, so a session that runs for a
week becomes a series of manageable files instead of one that grows
without limit. \c{0} switches it off.

\b This works only if the \e{file name} changes with time. Put \c{&T}
in it, as in \c{kitty_&H_&T.log}.

\b If the name has no time-varying code, KiTTY \e{declines} to rotate
and says so in the Event Log, because reopening the same name would
overwrite the log rather than rotate it.

The log file name's own substitutions are \c{&H} host name, \c{&P}
port, \c{&Y} \c{&M} \c{&D} year/month/day, \c{&T} time as HHMMSS, and
\c{&&} for a literal \c{&}. Characters that are illegal in a Windows
file name are replaced automatically, so an IPv6 address in \c{&H}
cannot produce an unusable name.

\H{kitty-cfg-scripting} The Scripting panel

\S{kitty-cfg-loginscript} \q{Login script}

A list of expect/send pairs - wait for this text, then send that -
carried out when the session opens. It is stored in the session, so it
travels with it; \c{-loginscript} loads one from a file on the command
line, and the button beneath the box fills the box from a file so what
is saved is what you can see.

\H{kitty-cfg-broadcast} The Broadcast panel

\S{kitty-cfg-sendcmd} \q{Accept broadcast messages for this session}

One KiTTY can type a line into another's session, as if it had been
typed at the keyboard - and a trailing Return means the far end
\e{runs} it. That is what makes a session scriptable from outside:
\c{kitty.exe -sendcmd}, or the \c{/command} control message, says one
thing to twenty servers at once.

It is dangerous for exactly the reason it is useful: the twenty include
whatever production session happens to be open. So a window listens
only when it has been armed, and only to messages meant for it:

\b \e{Per session}: this checkbox. Off by default.

\b \e{Per installation}: \c{sendcmdmode=yes} under \c{[KiTTY]} in
\c{kitty.ini} sets the state windows start in. The window menu's
\q{Tools > Accept broadcast} flips the window you are looking at; it is
the same switch seen from two places, so the configuration box shows
what the menu did and the other way round.

\b \e{Which KiTTYs hear each other}: the broadcast key below.

\e{Sending} is done from the command line:

\c kitty.exe -sendcmd "df -h"
\c kitty.exe -sendcmdkey mygroup -sendcmd "df -h"

The first form broadcasts with this installation's own key, the second
aims the broadcast at sessions carrying the key \c{mygroup}
(\c{-sendcmdkey} may stand on either side of \c{-sendcmd}). The
sending KiTTY delivers the line and exits; a trailing meaning of
\q{run it} comes from the receiving shell seeing Return.

\e{Why a broadcast can look like it does nothing}: THREE locks must
all be open before any window types a line, and each exists so that no
single mistake can reach a production session -

\b the installation's master switch, \cw{sendcmdmode=yes} under
\cw{[KiTTY]} in \c{kitty.ini} - without it no window of this install
accepts anything;

\b the per-window arming - this checkbox, or \q{Tools > Accept
broadcast} on the window itself;

\b the key match - the broadcast's key (default: the sender
installation's own) must equal the key each receiving session listens
for. A portable stick and an installed KiTTY have \e{different}
generated keys, so they do not hear each other until you give them a
common key.

\S{kitty-cfg-broadcast-key} \q{Broadcast key}

Decides who a broadcast reaches. Each installation gets its own
generated key, so a portable KiTTY on a USB stick does not type into
the sessions of the copy installed on the same machine.

Give several sessions a key of their own and only those answer, even
when the installation's key would have matched: that is how a broadcast
is aimed at three servers instead of at everything on the desktop. The
field is an ordinary text box because the value exists to be copied
elsewhere; \q{Copy} puts it on the clipboard and \q{Clear} returns the
session to the installation's key.

\S{kitty-cfg-not-security} What the broadcast gate is not

\e{None of this is a security boundary.} Anything running under your
account can post the same message, and could reach your session by
other means regardless. What these settings decide is \e{when your
terminals accept it} - they keep a broadcast off the wrong terminal,
not an attacker off your machine.

\H{kitty-cfg-startup} The Startup panel

\S{kitty-cfg-launcher-hotkey} \q{Enable global hotkey for this session}

Opens this session from anywhere on the desktop with one key
combination, for example \c{Ctrl+Alt+K} or \c{Ctrl+Shift+F12}.

The hotkey is registered machine-wide, so it works while another
program has the focus - and it works only while \e{KiTTY Launcher} is
running, because the launcher is what holds the registration. If two
sessions claim the same combination the launcher says so with a
balloon, and the first registration wins.

\H{kitty-cfg-keyboard} The Keyboard panel

\S{kitty-cfg-altgr} \q{AltGr sends Alt}

On an international layout, \i{AltGr} is how the characters printed on
the front of the keys are typed: on a German layout \c{AltGr+Q} gives
\c{@} and \c{AltGr+E} gives a euro sign. Windows produces those by
treating AltGr as Ctrl+Alt, which is also how a terminal application
sees a plain \c{Alt+key}. A program inside the terminal therefore
cannot tell \q{I typed a euro sign} from \q{I pressed Alt+E}.

This setting chooses which of the two KiTTY sends:

\b \e{Off} (the default): AltGr composes characters, as the layout
intends. \c{AltGr+Q} sends \c{@}.

\b \e{On}: \c{AltGr+key} is delivered as \c{Alt+key}, so a program that
binds Alt shortcuts - Midnight Commander, Emacs, a menu driven by Alt -
sees them.

Turn it on only if you need those shortcuts and do not need the
layout's extra characters in the same session. The two cannot both
work, which is why this is a switch and not something KiTTY can decide
for you.

It is \e{not} the same as \q{AltGr acts as Compose key} on the same
panel (\k{config-compose}), which is PuTTY's own Compose feature: there
AltGr followed by two more keystrokes builds one character, and a
single AltGr keypress is unaffected.

\S{kitty-cfg-wordnav} \q{Word navigation (Left/Right arrows)}

Which modifier sends the escape sequence a shell binds to
\e{backward-word} / \e{forward-word}. PuTTY emits it on \c{Alt+Left} and
\c{Alt+Right}; KiTTY can send it on \c{Ctrl} instead, or on both.

This takes effect only when \q{Shift/Ctrl/Alt with the arrow keys}
(\k{config-sharrow}) is set to \e{xterm-style bitmap}, KiTTY's default.
In \q{Ctrl toggles application mode} the arrow-key modifiers are not
encoded at all, so there is nothing to remap.

\H{kitty-cfg-window} The Window panels

\S{kitty-cfg-winpos} \q{Open the window at a fixed position}

Opens this session's window where you say, instead of where Windows
would put it. Top and Left are in screen pixels.

It is the companion to \q{Remember window position} (\k{kitty-cfg-winpos-remember}):
that one follows the window as you move it, this one pins it. Both can
be set, and then the fixed position wins - an explicit instruction
beats a convenience. That order matters if you use both, because the
remembered position silently stops having any effect once a fixed one
is set.

A position that no longer exists cannot strand a window. Monitors are
unplugged and resolutions change, and a position saved against a screen
that is no longer there would otherwise open the window where you
cannot reach it; KiTTY checks the pinned spot against the monitors you
have now and moves the window onto the nearest one if it fits on none
of them.

\S{kitty-cfg-transparency} \q{Transparency}

Makes the whole window translucent, so what is behind it shows through.
The value runs from \c{0} (opaque) to \c{255} (invisible), and \c{-1}
disables transparency completely.

Transparency is a window property, not a terminal one: it dims the
text along with the background, so a high value costs legibility. If
what you wanted was a picture behind readable text, use a background
image instead (\k{kitty-cfg-bgimage}).

\S{kitty-cfg-icon} \q{Window icon}

Gives this session its own icon, so a taskbar full of terminals can be
told apart at a glance - production red, staging blue, and so on.

\q{Icon (from internal resources)} takes the number of one of the icons
built into KiTTY. \q{External icon file} takes a path to an \c{.ico}
file, which wins over the internal number when both are set. The icon
applies to the window, the taskbar and the tray.

\S{kitty-cfg-bgimage} \ii{Background image}

Draws a picture, or a colour gradient, behind the terminal text.

\b \q{Background Style} chooses between no image, an image, and the
gradient forms.

\b \q{Image file} takes the picture; a value of the form \c{#RRGGBB} is
read as a colour for the gradient styles instead of a file name.

\b \q{Opacity} decides how strongly the image shows through the
terminal background. A \e{negative} value with an image selected
produces a gradient over the image.

\b \q{Slideshow} changes the picture every N seconds, cycling through
the images in the same directory as the one named.

\b \q{Image placement} positions the picture - centred, tiled,
stretched, or at the absolute Left/Top given below it - and the last
setting says whether those coordinates are relative to the window or to
the desktop, which is what keeps a tiled desktop image aligned across
several windows.

Text is drawn over the image with the terminal's own colours, so a busy
picture at a high opacity is unreadable; that is a matter of taste, not
a limit KiTTY imposes.

\S{kitty-cfg-hyperlinks} \ii{Hyperlinks} in the terminal

KiTTY finds URLs in the terminal output and makes them clickable.

\b \q{Require Ctrl key to click hyperlinks} keeps ordinary clicking as
selection, which is what you want if you select text with the mouse
more often than you follow links.

\b \q{Underline hyperlinks} and \q{Show hand cursor when hovering}
control how a link announces itself.

\b \q{Use the default browser} opens the link with whatever Windows is
configured to use; clear it and name another program in \q{Other
browser} to send links somewhere specific.

\b \q{Use the default regular expression} uses KiTTY's own pattern for
what a URL looks like. Clear it to supply your own in \q{Custom regex}
- useful for making ticket numbers or host names clickable.

The pattern decides what KiTTY treats as a link, and therefore what it
will hand to the browser. A pattern that matches more than URLs is a
way to hand arbitrary text to a program, so keep custom patterns
narrow.

\H{kitty-cfg-remote-clipboard} The Remote clipboard panels

A program on the far end can ask the terminal to put something on your
\i{clipboard} (\i{OSC 52}), and can ask to read it back. That is
genuinely useful over SSH - \c{tmux} and \c{neovim} copy this way - and
it is also a remote program touching a local resource, so KiTTY makes
both directions a policy rather than a fact.

\S{kitty-cfg-osc52-writes} \q{Writes - host sets your clipboard}

\b \e{Deny}: the request is ignored.

\b \e{Ask once per session}: the default. The first copy asks; the
answer then stands for the rest of the session, so tmux or neovim costs
one dialog and nothing afterwards.

\b \e{Allow}: writes are accepted silently, which is how terminals
without a policy behave.

Asking once per session is affordable precisely because it is not per
request. A prompt on every copy is the failure mode other terminals
have: their users report the warnings are annoying enough that everyone
turns them off, which leaves them with \e{Allow} and no thought given
to it.

\S{kitty-cfg-osc52-reads} \q{Reads - host asks for your clipboard}

The other direction, and the more sensitive one: your clipboard holds
whatever you last copied, which may be a password. The default is
\e{Deny}, and the only other setting is \e{Ask} - there is deliberately
no \q{Allow}, because a standing permission to read the clipboard is
not something a terminal setting should be able to grant silently.

\q{Only while this window has focus} refuses both directions while you
are working in another program, so a background session cannot act on
a clipboard you are using elsewhere.

\S{kitty-cfg-osc52-far2l} \q{far2l shared clipboard}

The far2l extension carries the same two operations for far2l's own
protocol. It follows the same policy values, and the focus rule above
covers it too - which is why that setting is named for the clipboard
rather than for OSC 52.

\S{kitty-cfg-osc52-limits} The Limits panel

Bounds on what a granted permission allows, so \q{Allow} does not mean
\q{unlimited}.

\b \q{Largest payload} caps one clipboard write, in megabytes.

\b \q{Most writes per second} rate-limits a program that copies in a
loop. \c{0} removes the limit.

\b \q{Grant offered} - in minutes and in requests - is how long an
answer of \q{yes} lasts. The grant ends at whichever runs out first.

\b \q{Shortest gap between reads} and \q{Most reads per window} bound
the read direction in the same way.

\b \q{Unanswered prompt expires} closes a question nobody answered, so
a session left alone does not sit with a permission dialog open, and
\q{Most prompts per ten seconds} stops a program from producing
dialogs faster than you can dismiss them.

\S{kitty-cfg-osc52-notices} The Notices panel

How KiTTY tells you a permission is live or has just been used. A
permission you have forgotten about is the thing that makes a standing
grant risky, so these are visible by design:

\b \q{Mark while a permission is live} puts a marker in the title bar
for as long as the grant lasts, and \q{Also mark a standing Allow}
extends that to a permission that came from the setting rather than
from a question you answered.

\b \q{Mark when the host actually uses it} shows a marker at the moment
of a read or write, and the field below it says for how long.

\b \q{Tint the title bar and border} colours the window frame instead
of writing in the title. Windows 11 only - earlier versions do not
offer the colours.

\b \q{Show tray notifications} raises a balloon as well, for the case
where the window is not on screen.

\H{kitty-cfg-transfers} File transfer panels

\S{kitty-cfg-kscp} The KSCP panel

Drag-and-drop uploads, and the \q{Start WinSCP} menu item, need to know
\e{where} on the far end to put a file.

\b \q{Track remote directory (OSC 7 shell integration)} sends them to
the shell's current directory instead of your home directory. KiTTY
reads that directory from the standard \i{OSC 7} sequence your shell
emits at every prompt (\c{ESC ] 7 ; file://host/path BEL}), validated
strictly as data: an absolute path, a whitelisted character set, never
executed. Two lines in your shell startup enable it; see the
\c{osc7-shell-integration} example shipped with KiTTY.

\b \q{Fixed remote upload directory} sends every upload to one place
instead. It is mutually exclusive with tracking above.

\b \q{KSCP options} are the flags handed to \c{kscp}; the default
\c{-r} is what makes a dropped folder upload recursively.

\b \q{Keep the transfer window open after success} leaves the transfer
window up so you can read what happened. Failures stay up regardless.

This replaced an older \q{send file to the current directory} feature
that worked by scanning the window title, which is a remote-code-execution
hole (CVE-2024-23749 in another terminal). Sessions that used it are
migrated to OSC 7 automatically.

\S{kitty-cfg-winscp-session} The WinSCP panel

What to hand \i{WinSCP} when it is started for this session.

\b \q{Preferred protocol} chooses SFTP or SCP.

\b \q{SFTP connect} overrides the target, as \c{[user@]hostname[:port]},
for the case where the file-transfer host is not the session's host.

\b \q{WinSCP additional options} and \q{additional rawsettings} are
passed to WinSCP verbatim - the second is WinSCP's own \c{-rawsettings}
mechanism, for settings it has no command-line switch for.

\b \q{Shell} names the remote shell to use, in SCP mode only.

Where WinSCP is \e{installed} is not here: that is a property of the PC
and lives on \k{kitty-cfg-external-tools}.

\S{kitty-cfg-zmodem-session} The ZModem panel

\i{ZModem} transfers files through the terminal session itself, with no
second connection - which is what makes it work through a jump host
where SCP does not.

The session settings are the options handed to \c{rz} (receive) and
\c{sz} (send), and the folder downloads are written to. \c{Ctrl+X}
aborts a transfer that has begun. The \e{paths} to \c{rz} and \c{sz}
are per PC and live on \k{kitty-cfg-external-tools}.

\H{kitty-cfg-knocking} \ii{Port knocking}

A sequence of connection attempts that a firewall watches for, and
opens the real port only after seeing it. KiTTY performs the sequence
before connecting, so the session opens without a separate tool.

The field is a comma-separated list of \c{port:protocol} knocks.
Protocols are \c{tcp} and \c{udp}, and \c{s} is a pause rather than a
knock, so

\c 2001:tcp, 1:s, 2002:udp

knocks 2001 over TCP, waits a second, then knocks 2002 over UDP.

\H{kitty-cfg-comment} The Comment panel

A free-text note stored with the saved session: what the machine is
for, who owns it, the change that had you connect to it.

It travels with the session through \q{Export all} / \q{Import all} and
survives a rename. It is never sent to the host, and KiTTY shows it
only on this panel - it is a note for you, not a label the session list
displays.

\H{kitty-cfg-application} The Application tab

The configuration box has two tabs. \q{Session} holds everything that
belongs to the session you are editing; \q{Application} holds the
settings that belong to this \e{installation} of KiTTY and apply to
every session it opens.

The split exists because those settings had nowhere else to live. A
named proxy, the path to WinSCP, or whether KiTTY checks for updates
were never properties of a connection, and keeping them among the
session settings meant saving a session to change something that had
nothing to do with it.

Application settings are saved as you change them - there is no
\q{Apply} for them - except on the two panels that hold an edit until
you press \q{Save}, which say so themselves: \k{kitty-cfg-named-proxies}
and \k{kitty-cfg-host-cas}.

\S{kitty-cfg-named-proxies} \ii{Named proxies}

A named proxy is a proxy or jump host defined once and used by name in
any number of sessions (\k{kitty-cfg-proxy-override}). Change the
definition - a jump host moves, a password rotates - and every session
that names it follows, because the sessions record the name, not the
settings.

\b \q{Name} picks the definition to edit, or takes a new name to create
one.

\b \q{Type} is the proxy protocol. Besides SOCKS, HTTP, Telnet and a
local command, the three SSH types make the definition a reusable
\e{jump host}: port forwarding, executing a command, or invoking a
subsystem.

\b \q{Name/IP} and \q{Port} are the proxy itself. A port you have not
typed follows the type - choosing SOCKS 5 fills in 1080, HTTP 3128, SSH
22 - and once you type one, the type stops touching it.

\b \q{.. this is ..} settles what the host field means. PuTTY has
always tried it as the name of a \e{saved session} first and only then
as a hostname, which is convenient and is also how a jump host that
happens to share a name with a saved session silently drags that whole
session's configuration - including its own proxy - into your
connection. Say which one it is here, per definition, or leave it at
the first entry to follow \c{namedproxy} in \c{kitty.ini}.

\b \q{Username} and \q{Password} are the proxy's own credentials.
\q{Show password} reveals what you typed. The password is stored
protected at rest, like a session's.

\b \q{Command to send} is the remote command or subsystem for the
Telnet, Local and SSH execute/subsystem types.

\b \q{Exclude Hosts/IPs} lists destinations that bypass the proxy, and
\q{Consider proxying local host connections} decides whether
\c{localhost} goes through it.

\b \q{DNS lookup at proxy} says who resolves the name, and \q{Print
diagnostics} whether the proxy negotiation is shown in the terminal.

Nothing on this panel reaches the store until \q{Save}, and leaving the
panel with an unsaved edit asks first. \q{Delete} removes the
definition after confirming; sessions that named it fall back to their
own proxy settings.

\S{kitty-cfg-config-window} The Config window panel

Settings of the configuration window itself.

\b \q{Colours} is the light/dark theme, and it is \e{one setting for
the whole suite}: \c{kitty}, \c{kageant} and \c{kittygen} all read it.
Dark mode needs Windows 10 1809 or newer, which is where Windows gained
the dark control themes it is built on. A change applies to windows
opened afterwards - this window keeps the colours it opened with,
because a theme is applied to a window when it is created.

\b \q{Category tree opens showing} sets how much of the tree is
expanded when the box opens: the top level only, or everything.

\b \q{Window height} and \q{Window width}, in pixels, open the box at a
size of your choosing; blank means the default. They take effect on the
window that is already open as well, and dragging the window's edge
writes them back, so the size you leave it at is the size it returns
with.

\b \q{Come back to this window instead of exiting} makes closing a
terminal return to the configuration box rather than end the program.
It applies to any terminal, even one that never connected. Closing the
configuration window itself still exits, and nothing comes back at
shutdown.

\S{kitty-cfg-sessionlist} The Session parameter panel

How the saved-session list behaves.

\b \q{Length, in rows} is how many sessions the list shows at once,
seven or more. It is built into the panel's layout, so it takes effect
the next time a configuration box is opened.

\b \q{Show \q{Default Settings} in the list} keeps the defaults entry
visible. Quick connect needs it: loading \q{Default Settings} is how
you get back to that mode.

\b \q{Show folders as rows, not a drop-down} lists the folders in the
session list itself instead of in a drop-down above it, which is worth
it once you have more than a handful.

\b \q{Search the list as you type} filters the list from the keyboard.

\b \q{Open on the last used session} starts each KiTTY on the session
you used last. Off, KiTTY starts in \e{quick connect}: every window
opens on \q{Default Settings} with the cursor already in \q{Host Name},
so you type an address and press Enter.

\b \q{Double-click a session to} chooses what a double-click in the
list does - load the session, or open it.

\b \q{Show the proxy chooser} controls whether sessions are offered the
named-proxy droplist at all. \q{Never} also hides the \q{Edit} button
beside it; the definitions themselves stay on
\k{kitty-cfg-named-proxies}, so turning the chooser off does not take
away the only place they can be edited.

\S{kitty-cfg-external-tools} The External tools panel

KiTTY drives three programs it does not contain: \i{WinSCP} for a
graphical file transfer of the session you are on, and \c{rz} / \c{sz}
for ZModem transfers inside the terminal.

Where those are installed is a property of this PC, not of a
connection - the same session opened on your laptop and on a
colleague's machine should not need two different paths - so the paths
are kept in \c{kitty.ini} and shared by every session. The settings
that \e{are} per session stay on the session's own panels:
\k{kitty-cfg-winscp-session} and \k{kitty-cfg-zmodem-session}.

\S{kitty-cfg-security} The Security panel

\q{Warn when an unverified agent serves the keys}.

An SSH agent holds your private keys and signs with them on request,
and any program can offer to be that agent: it is a named pipe, and
whoever gets there first answers. A program in that position sees every
key request KiTTY makes.

A signed KiTTY checks who is answering and warns when the agent is not
a signed build it recognises. That is not a claim that the agent is
malicious - a self-built \c{kageant}, or another agent you chose, is
unverified too - it is KiTTY telling you \e{who} it is about to hand a
signing request to, at the moment it matters.

Turn the warning off if you deliberately use an agent that cannot be
verified. It is stored per installation, as \c{verifyagent} in
\c{kitty.ini}.

\S{kitty-cfg-missing-features} \q{Say in the terminal what this Windows cannot do}

KiTTY runs on old versions of Windows deliberately. The features that need a
recent one - dark mode, Windows Hello, per-monitor DPI scaling, keeping
secrets encrypted in memory - are looked for while it starts rather than
demanded of the loader, so a Windows without them still runs KiTTY. Those
features simply do not happen.

That creates a bad failure: a feature which quietly never works looks exactly
like a setting that will not stick. So KiTTY says which ones they are.

\b One line in the terminal when a session opens, naming the features that are
unavailable. This checkbox is what turns that line off, and it is worth
turning off once you have read it - the answer is a property of the machine
and will not change.

\b The full list in the \i{Event Log}: each missing entry point and the DLL it
was expected in, beside the feature it powers. This is written whether the
checkbox is set or not. The checkbox governs the interruption, not the record.

It reports what was \e{looked up and not found}, so it also covers the things
that are not about age: a machine without \c{secur32.dll} loses GSSAPI single
sign-on, and one whose Winsock has no \c{getaddrinfo} resolves names over IPv4
only - which otherwise shows up as a host that is simply unreachable.

Stored per installation, as \c{warnmissingfeatures} in \c{kitty.ini}.

\S{kitty-cfg-host-cas} The Certificate Authorities panel

A \i{certificate authority} signs host keys for a whole estate, so a
new or rebuilt server is accepted without anyone checking its
fingerprint by hand - \q{the host key is not cached} stops happening
every time a machine is replaced.

\b \q{Name} identifies the CA record.

\b The public key is the CA's own, from whoever runs it.

\b \q{Valid hosts} is an \e{expression}, not a list: a wildcard such as
\c{*.example.com}, several of them joined with \c{||}, and \c{port:22}
to narrow it further. It is what keeps a CA for one estate from
vouching for hosts in another, so it is worth being precise here rather
than writing \c{*}.

Records are stored per user and work in portable mode. Nothing is
stored until \q{Save}. The full how-to, including the OpenSSH server
side, is at
\W{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY/blob/kitty-0.85/docs/SSH-CERTIFICATES.md}{docs/SSH-CERTIFICATES.md}.

\S{kitty-cfg-workplace} The Workplace proxy panel

One switch that sends \e{every} connection through a chosen proxy,
whatever each session stores - for the laptop that is behind a
corporate proxy at the office and directly on the network at home.

\b \q{Proxy for everything} names the definition to use
(\k{kitty-cfg-named-proxies}).

\b \q{Switch off after} disarms it again by itself, so an evening at
home does not start with twenty sessions failing through a proxy that
is not there.

\b \q{Switch on} arms it. While it is on, every connection uses that
proxy.

\e{No session is changed.} The mode is an override, not an edit: each
session's own Proxy panel stays exactly as it was and stays editable,
and switching the mode off returns every session to its own settings.

\S{kitty-cfg-migration} The Migration panel

Lists the sessions found in an older KiTTY's registry hive
(\c{9bis.com}) or in PuTTY's, and imports the ones you select into
KiTTY's own store. The panel appears only when such a hive holds
sessions.

\b \q{Show / edit / delete old PuTTY or KiTTY sessions} makes those
sessions visible in the saved-session list, where they can be opened,
edited and deleted. The setting behind the checkbox
(\c{showforeignsessions} in \c{kitty.ini}) actually has \e{three}
states, one more than a checkbox can show: until you touch the box it
stands on \c{auto}, which shows the old sessions only while this KiTTY
has none of its own, so they retire themselves once you do. Ticking or
clearing the box replaces \c{auto} with a firm \c{yes} or \c{no} - to
get \c{auto} back, edit the ini line. See also
\k{kitty-cfg-old-sessions}.

\b The list shows every session in both hives with the store it came
from. Pick one or more and press \q{Import selected Sessions}.

An import \e{copies}:

\b The old store is never changed. The original stays where it was, and
the same session can be imported again.

\b An existing name is never overwritten. The copy is called
\c{work (PuTTY)} or \c{work (old KiTTY)}, numbered if that name is
taken as well.

\b Settings this version no longer has are not carried over, and the
import names them when it finishes.

A session is imported by being \e{loaded and saved}, not by copying its
registry key, so what arrives is a session of KiTTY's own in KiTTY's
own formats - an old KiTTY's stored password, in particular, is decoded
on the way in and re-protected on the way out.

Two settings are deliberately left behind:

\b \c{OSC52WarnBeforeClipboardSync} meant \q{warn before a host writes
to the clipboard}, so its usual value of \e{off} meant hosts could
write to it silently. This version asks once per session instead
(\k{kitty-cfg-osc52-writes}), and honouring the old value would switch
remote clipboard writes on for every session you import.

\b \c{CheckUpdateStartup} belongs to the installation now
(\k{kitty-cfg-updates}), not to a session.

\c{SaveWindowPos} \e{is} carried over: it is called \c{SetWindowPos}
now, and a session that still has the old name is read through it, on
an import and on an ordinary load. A pinned position that is off-screen
is moved onto the nearest monitor (\k{kitty-cfg-winpos}), so an old
session cannot put a window out of reach.

\S{kitty-cfg-updates} The Updates panel

\b \q{Check for updates when KiTTY starts} looks for a newer release
and shows a one-line notice in the terminal when a session opens. It is
a property of the installation - \c{checkupdate} in \c{kitty.ini} - not
of a session.

\b \q{Check for updates now} asks immediately and reports either way,
which is also how you confirm the check can reach the network at all.

\H{kitty-cfg-more-session} Further session settings

\S{kitty-cfg-save-on-exit} \q{Save settings on exit}

Writes this session back to the store when its window closes, so a
change made through \q{Change Settings} during the session is kept
without saving by hand. Off by default: with it on, a change you made
to try something out is saved as well.

\S{kitty-cfg-scriptfile} Running a script file

A script file is a plain text file whose lines are typed into the
session for you, one after another, as if you typed them - the RuTTY
mechanism, and the right tool when the same sequence must go into many
hosts. \e{Everything on this panel acts only while a script is
running}: without a script, none of these fields does anything.

A script \e{starts} in one of two ways: automatically when the session
connects, if \q{Run the script on connect} is ticked and \q{Script
file} names the file; or on demand in a live session from the system
menu, \q{Tools > Send recorded script}, which asks for the file. Only
one script runs at a time, and every start, stop and timeout is
reported in the Event Log.

The simplest possible script is just the commands, one per line:

\c uname -a
\c df -h
\c exit

Sent like that, lines go out on a timer alone. The panel's real value
is making the script \e{wait for the host}:

\b \q{Wait for a prompt before sending} is the master switch for
waiting. With it on, the next line is sent only after the host's
output \e{ends with} the \q{Wait-for text} - your prompt's tail, for
example \c{$\_} or \c{>\_} (note the trailing space most prompts
have). This is what lets a script survive a slow login or a command
that takes a minute: nothing is typed into a prompt that is not there
yet.

\b \q{Except for first command} belongs to that switch: the FIRST line
is sent immediately, without waiting for a prompt first. Use it when
the host greets you with something that is not your prompt - a menu, a
\q{press enter to continue} - and the first line is what answers it.

\b \q{Halt-on text}: if the host's output ever contains this text
while the script runs, the script \e{stops at once} and says so in the
Event Log. It is the emergency brake - set it to the failure word your
commands produce, e.g. \c{denied} or \c{No such file}, and a script
cannot keep typing into a session where step three already failed.
Leave it empty and nothing stops the script early.

\b \q{Timeout} bounds every wait: if the wait-for text has not arrived
after this many seconds, the script gives up entirely (\q{script
timeout !} in the Event Log) rather than hang for ever. \q{Line delay}
and \q{Character delay} pace the typing itself, for hosts that drop
input sent faster than a human types.

A line's wait can also be set \e{from inside the file}, per line:

\b \q{Start of condition/comment line} names one character, \c{:} by
default. With \q{Use conditions from file} OFF, every line starting
with it is simply a \e{comment} and is skipped.

\b With \q{Use conditions from file} ON, a line starting with that
character is a \e{condition}: it names the wait-for text for the
\e{next line only}, overriding the global \q{Wait-for text} for that
one line. A line starting with the character \e{doubled} (\c{::}) is a
comment. Lines with no condition of their own still use the global
wait-for text, and \q{Halt-on text} stays armed throughout - the
switch adds per-line conditions, it disables nothing.

\c :login:
\c root
\c :assword:
\c wrong-example
\c :$
\c uname -a

\b \q{CR/LF translation} decides the line ending each line is sent
with - \q{Off} sends the file's own endings, \q{no LF} strips the
linefeed, \q{CR} sends carriage return only (what most interactive
hosts expect), \q{Rec} the recorded form.

A worked example ships as
\W{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY/blob/kitty-0.85/docs/examples/logon-script.ksh}{docs/examples/logon-script.ksh}.
The whole engine can be disabled machine-wide with \cw{[KiTTY]
scriptmode=no} in \c{kitty.ini}; the \e{login script} on
\e{Connection > Login} is a different, simpler mechanism
(\k{kitty-cfg-loginscript}) that needs none of this panel.

\S{kitty-cfg-autologin} \q{Auto-login password} and \q{Auto-command}

The password KiTTY sends when the host asks for one, and a command it
sends once the shell is there.

The password is stored \e{protected at rest}: DPAPI in registry mode,
or under the store's master password in a portable store. It is not
kept in the clear, and \q{Show password} is the only way to see it
again.

An automatic password is a convenience with a cost: anything that can
read your account can ask KiTTY to use it, and a host you did not
expect will be sent it just the same. Prefer key authentication where
you can, and keep automatic passwords for machines where a key is not
possible.

\q{Auto-command after login} is sent once, after authentication. It is
the place for \c{sudo -i} or attaching to a \c{tmux} session, not for a
sequence - use a script file (\k{kitty-cfg-scriptfile}) for that.

\S{kitty-cfg-antiidle} \q{Anti-idle string}

Text KiTTY sends to the host at intervals to stop an idle session being
closed by a firewall or an idle timeout on the far end. It is sent as
if typed, so choose something harmless - a space and a backspace, or a
newline at a shell prompt.

It is not the same as PuTTY's keepalives (\k{config-keepalive}), which
send protocol-level packets the far end never shows to a program. Use
keepalives first; an anti-idle string is for the case where the thing
timing you out is watching the \e{session}, not the connection.

\S{kitty-cfg-reconnect} \q{Attempt to reconnect}

\b \q{... on system wakeup} re-opens the session after the machine
resumes from sleep, where the connection is dead but the window is
still there.

\b \q{... on connection failure} re-opens it after the connection drops
for another reason.

A reconnect re-runs the session's automation - the login script, the
auto-command - because the far end is a fresh session that has none of
the earlier state.

KiTTY does \e{not} re-dial after an authentication failure. A password
or key the server rejected will be rejected again, and retrying it is
how an account gets locked out.

\S{kitty-cfg-keypin} \q{Pinned key fingerprint}

A session that authenticates with a private key file can record that
file's SHA256 fingerprint. Every connection then checks the file
against the record \e{before} offering the key or asking for its
passphrase, so a file that has been replaced or pointed somewhere else
is refused - with both fingerprints named in the terminal and the Event
Log - rather than used.

\q{Record fingerprint of the key file} fills the field in from the key
you have configured; it reads only the public half, so no passphrase is
involved. Clearing the field switches the check off, which is the
default.

The pin is always over the key itself, never over a detached
certificate, so renewing a certificate from an SSH CA does not disturb
it. The command-line tools honour the pin of a session they load.

\S{kitty-cfg-named-proxy-buttons} \q{Edit named proxies} and \q{Load named proxy pre-sets}

Two ways into the named-proxy definitions from a session's own Proxy
panel. \q{Edit named proxies} opens the panel where they live
(\k{kitty-cfg-named-proxies}). \q{Load named proxy pre-sets} copies one
definition's settings \e{into this session}, which is the opposite of
choosing it by name (\k{kitty-cfg-proxy-override}): the session then
has its own copy and stops following the definition.

\S{kitty-cfg-dynports} \q{Print dynamic ports in window title}

Shows the local ports of dynamic (SOCKS) forwardings in the window
title, so a session set up as a tunnel says which port it is listening
on without looking it up in the Event Log.

\H{kitty-cfg-more-terminal} Further terminal and window settings

\S{kitty-cfg-printclip} \q{Print to clipboard instead of printer}

Sends what the host prints (\k{config-printing}) to the Windows
clipboard instead of to a printer. It is the practical form of
terminal printing: a host that emits a report to \q{the printer} hands
it to you to paste somewhere.

\S{kitty-cfg-crlf} \q{Enter key sends CR LF}

Makes the Enter key send both characters instead of the one the
terminal setting calls for. Some hosts, and some line-based programs,
expect the pair; on a normal Unix host this is not wanted.

\S{kitty-cfg-fgbell} \q{Put window in foreground on bell}

Brings the window to the front when the host rings the bell
(\k{config-bell}), so a long job that finishes with a bell fetches your
attention rather than waiting for you to look.

\S{kitty-cfg-nofocusrep} \q{Disable focus reporting}

Stops KiTTY telling the host when the window gains or loses focus.
Programs that use it - editors that redraw or save on focus change -
stop doing so; the reason to turn it off is a program that misbehaves
on the reports, or leaks them as stray characters at the prompt.

\S{kitty-cfg-wheel} \q{Lines scrolled per wheel turn}

How far one notch of the mouse wheel scrolls the terminal. The system
default is three.

\S{kitty-cfg-behaviour} Window behaviour

\b \q{Send to tray on startup}, \q{Maximize on startup} and \q{Full
screen on startup} decide the state the window opens in. Sending to the
tray at startup is how a session that exists to hold a tunnel stays out
of the way.

\b \q{Switch KiTTY windows with Ctrl + TAB} moves between KiTTY windows
from the keyboard. Turn it off if a program inside the terminal wants
\c{Ctrl+Tab} for itself.

\b \q{System menu (off hides all buttons)} and \q{Allow closing (also
disables the X and Alt+F4)} remove the window's own controls. They are
for a KiTTY embedded in something else, or on a kiosk, where the window
must not be closed or moved by whoever is using it - not a security
measure, since the process can still be ended.

\b \q{Minimize button} and \q{Maximize button} take away those buttons
individually.

\S{kitty-cfg-winpos-remember} \q{Remember window position (per monitor layout)}

Re-opens each session where you last left its window. The position is
remembered \e{per monitor layout}, so docking a laptop - or unplugging
a screen - does not drag windows onto a monitor that is no longer
there: each arrangement of screens keeps its own set of positions.

A fixed position (\k{kitty-cfg-winpos}) wins over this, because it is
an explicit instruction rather than a convenience.

\S{kitty-cfg-clipcmd} Running the clipboard as a command

The window menu can run what is on the clipboard as a command in the
session. It saves pasting a long command line into a shell, and it is
also a way to run something you have not read, so it is guarded:

\b \q{Confirm before running the clipboard as a command} shows what is
about to be sent and waits for your answer. Leave it on.

\b \q{Show a tray notification after running a clipboard command}
reports afterwards, for the case where the window is not on screen.

\S{kitty-cfg-colour-extra} \q{Colour underlined text} and \q{Colour selected text}

Give underlined text, and the current selection, colours of their own
rather than the terminal's defaults. Both are set on the same panel as
the rest of the palette (\k{config-colours}).
